{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California tenant screening fee cap for 2026","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant no more than the state screening fee cap, may use the fee only to obtain information such as a consumer credit report, and must refund any unused portion and refund the fee to an applicant not selected.","key_value":"$68.96 maximum fee for 2026","coverage_conditions":"No building age, unit or owner limit is stated; the cap and refund rules apply to landlords who charge screening fees to rental applicants.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a landlord charges a screening fee to an applicant; the fee must be refunded to an applicant not selected or when no unit is available.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"A landlord who reviews applications in the order received, gives the unit to the first qualified applicant and charges no fee to applicants not considered need not refund the fee.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026","valid_through":"2026","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"The maximum tenant screening fee for 2026 is $68.96","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Berkeley screening fee rights statement and renewal fee ban","requirement":"An owner who charges an application screening fee must give the applicant a tenant screening fee rights statement and the state fee cap; non-refundable fees to renew a tenancy or change roommates are barred and conflicting lease terms are unenforceable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Chapter 13.78 applies to all residential rental agreements regardless of any contractual language in any rental agreement or lease to the contrary.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when an owner charges a fee to process a rental application, and to existing tenants renewing a tenancy or adding or replacing a roommate.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.78","quoted_span":"An owner of residential rental property (or the owner’s agent) cannot charge a non-refundable fee to any existing tenant for the purpose of renewing a tenancy, in whole or in part","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reusable screening report and credit report copy rules","requirement":"A landlord must use a reusable screening report provided by the tenant, and must give the prospective tenant a copy of any consumer credit report obtained, within 7 days of the landlord receiving it.","key_value":"7 days to provide the credit report copy","coverage_conditions":"No building age, unit or owner limit is stated; applies to landlords screening prospective residential tenants.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a tenant provides a reusable screening report, or when the landlord obtains a consumer credit report on an applicant.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"If the tenant provides a reusable screening report, the landlord must use it.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
